course: dictionary
lesson: 951
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning is right. «He had a bloody nose after the fall.» Bloody means covered in blood or bleeding here, because the nose is hurt.
explain: «The knight fought a bloody battle.» Here bloody means full of killing and injury. Read the rest of the sentence: a battle is a fight, so the clues point to the meaning about harm and violence.
explain: Bloody has a third meaning in Britain, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland. There it is a rude, mild swear word that people use to show strong feeling, such as anger, surprise or pain. Softer words, like «bloomin'» in «bloomin' rain», are often used instead.
explain: Check the clues. If bloody is about blood, a wound or a fight, it is the first two meanings. If it is just adding strong feeling, it is the rude sense, and you should never use it in school writing.
fact: Bloody means covered in blood or bleeding, as in «a bloody nose».
fact: Bloody can also describe a battle with a lot of killing and injury, as in «a bloody battle».
fact: In Britain, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland, bloody is a rude, mild swear word that shows strong feeling.
fact: People use softer words instead of bloody, such as «bloomin'», «flipping» and «ruddy».
fact: Bloody is not polite, and it is never used in school writing.
exercise: «He had a bloody nose after the fall.» What does bloody mean here? | * Covered in blood or bleeding | Very clean and shiny | Full of laughter | Cold and wet
exercise: Which softer word is often used instead of the swear word bloody? | Tiny | Silent | Happy | * Bloomin'
exercise: What does «a bloody battle» mean? | A battle won with ease | A quiet parade | * A battle with a lot of killing and injury | Happy and friendly
test: Dad shouts «bloody hell» when he stubs his toe. What kind of word is bloody here? | Part of a fairy tale | * A rude, mild swear word that shows strong feeling | A kind of weather | A word for blood
test: Which advice is right for school writing? | Use bloody only in a poem | Use bloody in a story to sound grown up | Use bloody when you are very happy | * Never use bloody in school writing
exam: Ella hears «bloody hell!» from a man who has stubbed his toe. What does bloody show? | A bleeding wound | A battle | A cold winter | * Strong feeling, such as pain or anger
